---
import SiteLayout from "../layouts/SiteLayout.astro";
import AtlasPreview from "../components/AtlasPreview.astro";
import PageHero from "../components/PageHero.astro";
import IconCard from "../components/IconCard.astro";
---

<SiteLayout
  title="Features"
  active="/features/"
  description="Explore AtlasOS desktop, education workflows, diagnostics, and custom UEFI boot frontend."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="The Atlas experience"
    title="A Linux environment shaped around the lesson."
    text="AtlasOS combines a classroom-oriented desktop with familiar Linux foundations. The project is actively developing, and each capability has a clearly documented scope."
    image="/AtlasOS/images/atlas-landscape.png"
    imageAlt="AtlasOS blue mountain landscape artwork"
  />
  <section class="page-content">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="content-block">
        <span class="eyebrow">Product foundations</span><h2>
          Small, practical pieces that work together.
        </h2><p>
          AtlasOS builds on existing Linux services and applications, adding an
          Atlas desktop and classroom-focused integration around them.
        </p><div class="feature-grid">
          <IconCard
            icon="▧"
            title="Education-focused environment"
            text="A home screen and navigation organized around common teaching actions and local resource shortcuts."
          /><IconCard
            icon="◈"
            title="Qt / QML desktop"
            text="A custom interface with system controls, lesson tools, settings, and device information."
          /><IconCard
            icon="⌁"
            title="Atlas Boot Manager"
            text="A Rust UEFI application that presents the Atlas boot experience and starts the documented boot backend."
          /><IconCard
            icon="◎"
            title="Interactive-board-first direction"
            text="Designed with classroom interactive boards in mind; current hardware evidence remains limited."
          /><IconCard
            icon="◌"
            title="Live Linux environment"
            text="A USB-bootable Live system for evaluation. An installer and in-place upgrade are not currently provided."
          /><IconCard
            icon="⌂"
            title="Open source"
            text="Atlas-owned source is published under MIT, with third-party components under their own terms."
          />
        </div>
      </div><div class="content-block split">
        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">Custom Boot Manager</span><h2>
            An Atlas welcome before Linux starts.
          </h2><p>
            The UEFI frontend is written in Rust and uses the system firmware
            graphics interface. In the validated normal path it starts the GRUB
            backend, then Linux, Casper Live, Plymouth, and the Atlas session.
          </p><a class="button button-primary" href="/AtlasOS/screenshots/"
            >See the visual preview →</a>
        </div><div class="gallery-image boot-preview">
          <div class="boot-card">
            <div class="boot-brand">▲ AtlasOS</div><div
              class="boot-choice active"
            >
              Start AtlasOS Live <span style="float:right">›</span>
            </div><div class="boot-choice">
              Advanced options <span style="float:right">›</span>
            </div><div class="boot-choice">
              System tools <span style="float:right">›</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div><div class="content-block split reverse">
        <div>
          <span class="eyebrow">Modern desktop experience</span><h2>
            Keep the next useful action close.
          </h2><p>
            Atlas desktop components provide lesson-oriented navigation,
            application shortcuts, audio and network status, and dock behavior
            integrated with the existing session.
          </p><p>
            What works can vary by device, connected hardware, and Live-session
            context.
          </p>
        </div><AtlasPreview compact={true} />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</SiteLayout>
